Core Low-Power Techniques in VLSI
Clock Gating (Dynamic Power Reduction): Disables the clock signal to idle circuit blocks, preventing unnecessary switching activity and saving substantial dynamic power. Often requires a latch to prevent glitches.
Power Gating (Static Leakage Reduction): Uses sleep transistors to switch off the power supply to idle circuit blocks, drastically reducing static leakage current.
Multi-Vt Design (Multi-Threshold CMOS): Employs high threshold voltage (HVT) transistors for non-critical paths to minimize leakage, while using low threshold voltage (LVT) transistors for timing-critical paths to maintain performance.
Multi-VDD Design (Voltage Islands): Operates different parts of the chip at varying supply voltages; non-critical logic runs at lower voltage, reducing both 
 components of power.
Dynamic Voltage and Frequency Scaling (DVFS): Adjusts the voltage and frequency dynamically based on the workload requirements, optimizing power usage without sacrificing performance when needed.
Operand Isolation: Prevents input operands from reaching combinational logic blocks when the result is not needed, minimizing switching activity.
Layout and Optimization Techniques: Includes using Grey coding for counters to minimize signal transitions and utilizing specialized routing techniques (like smart NDRs) to reduce capacitance. 
Low-Power Design Flow
System Level: Algorithm selection (e.g., using Gray code instead of binary) and energy-efficient architecture.
RTL Level: Clock gating, operand isolation, and power-aware logic optimization using tools like Synopsys Design Compiler.
Physical Level: Power gating insertion, Multi-Vt library usage, and optimized placement and routing. 
Key Terminology and Tools
UPF (Unified Power Format): Used to define power intent, such as isolation cells, level shifters, and power domains.
EDA Tools: Synopsys Power Compiler, Cadence Genus, and PrimePower are standard for power-aware synthesis and analysis.
Static Power: Power consumed due to leakage current even when the circuit is idle.
Dynamic Power: Power consumed during switching activity. 
Common Pitfalls
Clock Gating: Improper implementation can cause clock skew or race conditions.
Power Gating: Requires careful design of retention registers to save data before powering down. 
